\section{Introduction to Partial Evaluation}\label{sec:pe-intro}

\emph{Partial evaluation} is the art of specializing a program with respect to some of its inputs. Given a program $p$ that takes two arguments $s$ and $d$, a \emph{partial evaluator} produces a \emph{residual program} $p_s$ such that $p_s(d) = p(s, d)$ for all $d$. The residual program is specialized to the \emph{static} input $s$ and remains general in the \emph{dynamic} input $d$.

The promise of partial evaluation is extraordinary: by specializing an interpreter to a particular source program, we obtain a compiled version of that program. By specializing the partial evaluator to an interpreter, we obtain a compiler. By specializing the partial evaluator to itself, we obtain a compiler generator. These three results are the celebrated \emph{Futamura projections}.

This chapter develops the theory and practice of partial evaluation, from the basic definitions to the deep results about self-application and compiler generation.

\section{Partial Evaluation: Specialization of Programs}\label{sec:pe-basics}

\subsection{The Specialization Problem}

\begin{definition}[Partial Evaluation]\label{def:partial-eval}
Let $p : S \times D \to R$ be a program with static inputs of type $S$ and dynamic inputs of type $D$. A \emph{partial evaluator} is a function:
\[
\mathit{spec} : (S \times D \to R) \times S \to (D \to R)
\]
such that for all programs $p$ and static inputs $s$:
\[
\mathit{spec}(p, s)(d) = p(s, d) \quad \text{for all } d \in D
\]
The result $p_s = \mathit{spec}(p, s)$ is called the \emph{residual program} or \emph{specialized program}.
\end{definition}

The quality of a partial evaluator is measured not just by correctness (the equation above) but by the \emph{efficiency} of the residual program. A trivial partial evaluator that returns $\lambda d.\,p(s, d)$ is correct but useless---it performs no specialization. A good partial evaluator performs:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Constant propagation}: Known values are substituted for static variables.
  \item \textbf{Constant folding}: Operations on known values are computed at specialization time.
  \item \textbf{Dead code elimination}: Code that depends only on static inputs and whose result is unused is removed.
  \item \textbf{Loop unrolling}: Loops with static bounds are unrolled.
  \item \textbf{Function inlining}: Calls with static arguments are inlined and simplified.
  \item \textbf{Static dispatch}: Case analyses on static values are resolved.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Binding-Time Analysis}

Before specialization can begin, the partial evaluator must determine which inputs and intermediate values are \emph{static} (known at specialization time) and which are \emph{dynamic} (unknown, to be computed at run time). This is the role of \emph{binding-time analysis} (BTA).

\begin{definition}[Binding-Time Analysis]\label{def:bta}
A \emph{binding-time analysis} is a program analysis that assigns to each variable and intermediate value in a program a \emph{binding time}: either $\mathit{static}$ (known at specialization time) or $\mathit{dynamic}$ (computed at run time). The analysis must satisfy the constraint:
\begin{itemize}
  \item If any operand of an operation is dynamic, the result is dynamic.
  \item If all operands of an operation are static, the result is static (and the operation can be performed at specialization time).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Binding-time analysis is typically formulated as a dataflow analysis over a two-point lattice $\{\mathit{static} \sqsubseteq \mathit{dynamic}\}$:

