  \node[left=0.3cm of stat] {\small $\mathit{static}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The binding-time lattice: $\mathit{static} \sqsubseteq \mathit{dynamic}$}
\label{fig:bta-lattice}
\end{figure}

The analysis computes the \emph{least fixed point} of the constraint system, yielding the most precise (most static) classification consistent with the constraints.

\begin{example}[Binding-Time Analysis]\label{ex:bta}
Consider the program $p(n, x) = \mathit{if}\;n = 0\;\mathit{then}\;1\;\mathit{else}\;x * p(n-1, x)$, with $n$ static and $x$ dynamic.

\begin{itemize}
  \item $n$ is static (given).
  \item $x$ is dynamic (given).
  \item $n = 0$ is static (comparison of static values).
  \item $n - 1$ is static (arithmetic on static values).
  \item $x * p(n-1, x)$ is dynamic (involves dynamic $x$).
  \item The recursive call $p(n-1, x)$ is dynamic.
\end{itemize}

The result: the test $n = 0$ and the decrement $n - 1$ are static; the multiplication and recursive call are dynamic. The partial evaluator will unroll the recursion on $n$.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Specialization Algorithm}

\begin{tomealgorithm}[H]
\caption{Basic Partial Evaluator}
\label{alg:basic-pe}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{specialize}{$p$, $\bar{s}$}
  \State $\rho \gets \mathit{BTA}(p, \bar{s})$ \Comment{Binding-time analysis}
  \State $\mathit{residual} \gets \epsilon$ \Comment{Empty residual program}
  \State $\mathit{memo} \gets \emptyset$ \Comment{Memoization table}
  \State \Call{Residualize}{$p$, $\rho$, $\bar{s}$, $\mathit{residual}$, $\mathit{memo}$}
  \State \Return $\mathit{residual}$
\EndFunction
\Statex
\Function{Residualize}{$e$, $\rho$, $\sigma$, $\mathit{residual}$, $\mathit{memo}$}
  \State \textbf{if} $e$ is a constant $c$ \textbf{and} $\rho(e) = \mathit{static}$ \textbf{then}
    \State \Return $\mathrm{eval}(c, \sigma)$
  \State \textbf{else if} $e$ is $e_1 \oplus e_2$ \textbf{and} $\rho(e_1) = \rho(e_2) = \mathit{static}$ \textbf{then}
    \State \Return $\mathrm{eval}(e_1, \sigma) \oplus \mathrm{eval}(e_2, \sigma)$
  \State \textbf{else if} $e$ is $\mathit{if}\;e_0\;\mathit{then}\;e_1\;\mathit{else}\;e_2$ \textbf{and} $\rho(e_0) = \mathit{static}$ \textbf{then}
    \State \textbf{if} $\mathrm{eval}(e_0, \sigma)$ \textbf{then} \Return \Call{Residualize}{$e_1$, $\rho$, $\sigma$, $\mathit{residual}$, $\mathit{memo}$}
    \State \textbf{else} \Return \Call{Residualize}{$e_2$, $\rho$, $\sigma$, $\mathit{residual}$, $\mathit{memo}$}
  \State \textbf{else}
    \State \Return $\mathrm{buildDynamic}(e, \rho, \sigma, \mathit{residual}, \mathit{memo})$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{tomealgorithm}

The key insight is that the partial evaluator \emph{simulates} the program on the static inputs while \emph{generating code} for the dynamic parts. When a conditional depends on a static value, it is resolved at specialization time---this is the source of loop unrolling and function inlining.

\subsection{Termination and the Online/Offline Distinction}

A fundamental challenge in partial evaluation is \emph{termination}. If the program being specialized contains loops or recursion over dynamic data, the partial evaluator must know when to stop unrolling and emit a residual loop.

\begin{definition}[Online vs.\ Offline Partial Evaluation]\label{def:online-offline-pe}
\begin{itemize}
  \item An \emph{offline} partial evaluator performs binding-time analysis as a separate phase before specialization. The binding times are fixed and guide the specializer.
  \item An \emph{online} partial evaluator interleaves binding-time analysis with specialization, making decisions dynamically based on the actual static values encountered.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Offline partial evaluators are simpler and more predictable but less precise: the BTA must over-approximate, classifying some values as dynamic that could have been determined static. Online partial evaluators are more precise but face termination challenges: they must detect when specialization is diverging and insert \emph{generalization} steps (widening) to force convergence.

\section{The Three Futamura Projections}\label{sec:futamura-projections}

\subsection{Historical Context}

Yukihiko Futamura published his landmark paper in 1971~\cite{futamura1971partial}, identifying three remarkable consequences of applying a partial evaluator to an interpreter. The results were largely ignored in the West until they were rediscovered and popularized by Jones, Gomard, and Sestoft in their 1993 book~\cite{jones1993partial}.

\subsection{The Setup}

Let $\mathit{interp}$ be an interpreter for a source language $L$:
\[
\mathit{interp} : \mathit{source} \times \mathit{input} \to \mathit{output}
\]
where $\mathit{interp}(s, d)$ computes the result of running source program $s$ on input $d$. Let $\mathit{spec}$ be a partial evaluator:
\[
\mathit{spec} : (A \times B \to C) \times A \to (B \to C)
\]

\subsection{The First Futamura Projection}\label{subsec:first-projection}

\begin{theorem}[First Futamura Projection]\label{thm:first-futamura}
Specializing an interpreter to a source program yields a compiled version of that program:
\[
\mathit{spec}(\mathit{interp}, s) = \mathit{compiled}_s
\]
where $\mathit{compiled}_s(d) = \mathit{interp}(s, d)$ for all $d$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the correctness of partial evaluation:
\[
\mathit{spec}(\mathit{interp}, s)(d) = \mathit{interp}(s, d)
\]
The residual program $\mathit{compiled}_s = \mathit{spec}(\mathit{interp}, s)$ takes only the dynamic input $d$ and produces the same result as the interpreter. Since the source program $s$ has been ``compiled in,'' the residual program is a compiled version of $s$.
\end{proof}

The first projection is the most intuitive: it says that partial evaluation of an interpreter is compilation. The specialization eliminates the overhead of interpreting (fetching instructions, dispatching, environment lookups) and produces a direct computation.

\subsection{The Second Futamura Projection}\label{subsec:second-projection}

\begin{theorem}[Second Futamura Projection]\label{thm:second-futamura}
Specializing a partial evaluator to an interpreter yields a compiler:
\[
\mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{interp}) = \mathit{compiler}
\]
where $\mathit{compiler}(s) = \mathit{spec}(\mathit{interp}, s)$ for all source programs $s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the correctness of partial evaluation applied to $\mathit{spec}$ itself:
\[
\mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{interp})(s) = \mathit{spec}(\mathit{interp}, s)
\]
The left side is the residual program $\mathit{compiler}$ applied to $s$. The right side is the specialized interpreter for $s$, which is $\mathit{compiled}_s$. So $\mathit{compiler}(s) = \mathit{compiled}_s$, meaning $\mathit{compiler}$ is indeed a compiler from $L$ to the target language.
\end{proof}

The second projection is remarkable: by specializing the partial evaluator to the interpreter, we obtain a \emph{compiler}---a function that takes any source program and produces compiled code. The compiler is generated automatically, without any explicit compiler-writing effort.

\subsection{The Third Futamura Projection}\label{subsec:third-projection}

\begin{theorem}[Third Futamura Projection]\label{thm:third-futamura}
Specializing a partial evaluator to itself yields a compiler generator:
\[
\mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{spec}) = \mathit{cogen}
\]
where $\mathit{cogen}(\mathit{interp}) = \mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{interp}) = \mathit{compiler}$ for any interpreter $\mathit{interp}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the correctness of partial evaluation:
\[
\mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{spec})(\mathit{interp}) = \mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{interp})
\]
The left side is $\mathit{cogen}$ applied to $\mathit{interp}$. The right side is $\mathit{compiler}$ (by the second projection). So $\mathit{cogen}(\mathit{interp}) = \mathit{compiler}$.
\end{proof}

The third projection is the most mind-bending: a partial evaluator applied to itself produces a \emph{compiler generator}---a tool that takes any interpreter and produces a compiler. This is \emph{self-application}, and it works because the partial evaluator is just a program, and partial evaluation can specialize any program, including itself.

