\subsection{Practical Challenges of Self-Application}

While the Futamura projections are elegant in theory, achieving them in practice is extraordinarily difficult. The challenges include:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Termination}: When $\mathit{spec}$ specializes itself, it must handle the general case of specializing an arbitrary program (which happens to be $\mathit{spec}$). Ensuring termination requires sophisticated generalization and widening strategies.
  \item \textbf{Self-representation}: The partial evaluator must be able to represent and manipulate its own data structures. This requires a careful encoding of the language in which $\mathit{spec}$ is written.
  \item \textbf{Efficiency}: The generated compiler must be efficient. Naive self-application produces a compiler that is correct but slow. Achieving a practical compiler requires extensive engineering.
  \item \textbf{Binding-time errors}: The BTA must correctly classify the self-application, which involves deeply nested recursion and complex data flow.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Self-Application and Compiler Generation}\label{sec:self-application}

\subsection{The Theory of Self-Application}

Self-application is possible because partial evaluation is a \emph{general-purpose} program transformation. The partial evaluator $\mathit{spec}$ is a function from programs to programs, and it can be applied to any program, including itself.

\begin{definition}[Self-Applicable Partial Evaluator]\label{def:self-applicable}
A partial evaluator $\mathit{spec}$ is \emph{self-applicable} if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\mathit{spec}$ can process programs written in the same language as $\mathit{spec}$ itself.
  \item $\mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, p)$ terminates for all programs $p$ in the language.
  \item The residual program $\mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, p)$ is in the target language and is correct.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The requirement that $\mathit{spec}$ can process its own source code is the critical constraint. It means the language of $\mathit{spec}$ must be expressive enough to describe interpreters, and the partial evaluator must handle all the constructs that interpreters use.

\subsection{Cogen: The Compiler Generator}

When self-application succeeds, the result is a \emph{compiler generator} $\mathit{cogen}$:

\begin{equation}
\mathit{cogen} = \mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{spec})
\end{equation}

This $\mathit{cogen}$ has the remarkable property that for \emph{any} interpreter $\mathit{interp}$:

\begin{equation}
\mathit{cogen}(\mathit{interp}) = \mathit{spec}(\mathit{spec}, \mathit{interp}) = \mathit{compiler}_{\mathit{interp}}
\end{equation}

The compiler $\mathit{compiler}_{\mathit{interp}}$ is generated automatically from the interpreter, with no manual compiler-writing effort.

\begin{insight}
The third Futamura projection reveals a deep connection between interpretation and compilation: \emph{every interpreter implicitly contains a compiler}. The partial evaluator makes this compiler explicit. This is not just a theoretical curiosity---it suggests that language implementers should write interpreters first (they are simpler and more maintainable) and then use partial evaluation to derive compilers.
\end{insight}

\subsection{The 1+1=1 Phenomenon}

A remarkable property of successful self-application is that the generated compiler generator is often \emph{no larger} than the original partial evaluator. This is because the specialization process eliminates the overhead of interpreting the partial evaluator, leaving a streamlined version. This ``1+1=1'' phenomenon was observed in the first successful self-applications.

\subsection{Successful Self-Applications}

Several research groups have achieved successful self-application:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cogen (1989)}: Dershowitz and colleagues produced a compiler generator by self-applying a partial evaluator for a simple functional language.
  \item \textbf{Mix (1990s)}: Jones and colleagues at the University of Copenhagen built the Mix system, which achieved self-application for a substantial subset of Scheme~\cite{jones1993partial}.
  \item \textbf{Similix (1990s)}: Bondorf and colleagues developed Similix, a self-applicable partial evaluator for first-order functional programs.
  \item \textbf{Jones' specializer (1993)}: A hand-crafted cogen for a simple language, demonstrating the feasibility of the approach.
\end{itemize}

\section{Practical Partial Evaluators}\label{sec:practical-pe}

\subsection{Mix: The Copenhagen Approach}

The Mix system~\cite{jones1993partial} was the most ambitious partial evaluation project. It was designed from the ground up to support self-application. Key design decisions included:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{First-order functional language}: Mix operated on a simple, first-order functional language, avoiding the complexities of higher-order functions and polymorphism.
  \item \textbf{Offline specialization}: Binding-time analysis was performed as a separate phase, ensuring predictable termination.
  \item \textbf{Tree-graph representation}: Programs were represented as \emph{program trees} with sharing (directed acyclic graphs), enabling efficient manipulation.
  \item \textbf{Minimal language}: The source language was kept small to simplify the self-representation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Cogen/Let: A Compiler Generator Approach}

The Cogen/Let approach~\cite{bondorf1991cogen} takes a different route to self-application. Instead of building a general-purpose partial evaluator and specializing it to itself, Cogen/Let constructs a \emph{compiler generator} directly by annotating the interpreter with \emph{binding-time improvements}.

The key idea is \emph{Lambda-dropping}: transforming higher-order programs into first-order form, and then annotating each expression with its binding time. The annotated interpreter is then processed by a \emph{multi-level intermediate language} that separates static and dynamic computations.

\subsection{Tempo and Schism}

\textbf{Tempo}~\cite{andersen1992tempo} was a partial evaluator for Standard ML, demonstrating that partial evaluation could be applied to a realistic, impure functional language. Key features included:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Support for side effects (references, I/O)
  \item Online binding-time analysis
  \item Generalization via \emph{homeomorphic embedding} to ensure termination
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Schism}~\cite{consel1993schism} extended Tempo with a more sophisticated treatment of data structures and control flow.

\subsection{Termination Control: Homeomorphic Embedding}

A critical innovation in practical partial evaluation was the use of \emph{homeomorphic embedding} as a termination criterion~\cite{jones1993partial}. The idea is to track the \emph{size} of expressions being specialized and stop unrolling when the expression grows too large.

\begin{definition}[Homeomorphic Embedding]\label{def:homeo-embed}
The \emph{homeomorphic embedding} relation $\trianglelefteq$ on expressions is defined inductively:
\begin{align}
e &\trianglelefteq e \\
e_1 &\trianglelefteq f(e_1, \ldots, e_n) \\
e_2 &\trianglelefteq f(e_1, \ldots, e_n) \\
e_i \trianglelefteq e'_i &\implies f(\ldots, e_i, \ldots) \trianglelefteq f(\ldots, e'_i, \ldots)
\end{align}
A specialization loop is terminated when the call being specialized \emph{embeds} a previously seen call.
\end{definition}

This criterion is more permissive than simple size bounds: it allows the expression to grow in width (more arguments) as long as it doesn't grow in depth (nested calls). This captures the intuition that specialization should unroll loops but not create infinitely nested structures.

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Stopping Unrolling Without Losing the Static Argument}

A recursive call can have a known argument and still be a poor candidate
for full evaluation at specialization time. For example, repeated shifts
by a dynamic offset can be unrolled when the repetition count is known.
A very large count would turn a small loop into a very large expression.
Stopping early does not require discarding the count or rejecting the
source program.

\textbf{Contract.} The pure source function \(F(n,x)\) adds the
runtime offset \(x\) to an accumulator initially zero, \(n\) times.
Specialization receives integers \(n,b\ge0\); the budget \(b\) caps the
number of additions emitted outside the generic helper. The residual
function must return \(F(n,x)\) for every runtime integer \(x\).
Arithmetic is exact and \(F\) is available in the residual environment.

\begin{sudocode}[Residualize a bounded prefix]
\Let{k}{\min(n,b)}
\Let{B}{\text{empty residual instruction buffer}}
\If{$k<n$}
  \To{Pass the remaining static count as a literal.}
  \State emit $a\gets F(n-k,x)$ into $B$
\Else
  \State emit $a\gets 0$ into $B$
\EndIf
\For{$i=1$ to $k$}
  \To{Each emitted addition still uses the runtime value.}
  \State emit $a\gets a+x$ into $B$
\EndFor
\State emit return $a$ into $B$
\State \Return residual function of $x$ with body $B$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} For \(n=5,b=2\), the residual program starts
with \(F(3,x)\) and then adds \(x\) twice. At \(x=4\), the
accumulator starts at \(12\), then reaches \(16\) and \(20\).
With budget zero it simply delegates to \(F(5,x)\); with
budget at least five it emits no helper call.

After \(j\) emitted additions execute, \(a=F(n-k+j,x)\);
the last value therefore equals the original result. The specializer
emits \(O(\min(n,b)+1)\) instructions, independent of the cost
of the residual helper call. This example caps code size, not
runtime cost. Extending this policy to an effectful recursion would
require preserving the order of prefix and suffix effects rather than
freely moving the helper call before the unrolled operations.

In \Cref{fig:visual-117}, the unexpanded count survives as a literal
in the helper call, while the runtime argument feeds every stage.

